-\section{Method}
-% TODO(agent): answer all four questions in SPEC. Amount tiers need their own n;
-% position must compare the same scenario family with only clause location moved;
-% quality and pruning contrasts must use matched cases; report exact-output compliance.
+\paragraph{Model and execution.}
+The subject is the frozen \modelFile{} artifact with SHA-256 \modelSha{}. We run it through \texttt{llama-completion} with greedy decoding, zero temperature, and a fixed seed. The complete manifest contains \manifestProbeCount{} probes, each stored as one prompt and one raw output. Existing outputs are skipped on rerun, so interrupted inference resumes by stable probe identifier.
 
+\paragraph{Scenario families.}
+Seeded code generates a company, target code, competing company and code, question, and filler for each family. The question asks for the renewal code needed to extend the target company's contract. Its wording exactly matches the competing clause, whereas the answer-bearing clause calls the same value a renewal identifier. A matched literal control reverses these phrasings while retaining the same filler.
+
+For each full-context family, we insert the answer clause near the start, middle, or end. Family generation does not use target position in any random seed. The competing clause is independently assigned to an earlier or later fractional depth and remains there across all three renders. Thus a paired position contrast changes only where the answer clause appears.
+
+\paragraph{Amount and quality.}
+The tokenizer, rather than a character estimate, determines prompt length. The three full-context tiers average \tierTwoTokens{}, \tierFourTokens{}, and \tierEightTokens{} tokens. Each tier balances topically related filler with unrelated sentences. Related filler consists of other companies, business records, and unrelated codes; unrelated filler describes places and events outside the contract domain. Quality pairs share the family, target position, question, and measured tier.
+
+The expensive final tier contains fewer families. Amount estimates therefore use only the \amountSharedEightCount{} cases shared by every tier, not the unequal full-tier totals. Quality estimates use \qualityTwoRelevantDenominator{}, \qualityFourRelevantDenominator{}, and \qualityEightRelevantDenominator{} pairs at the three tiers, respectively.
+
+\paragraph{Pruning and controls.}
+At the middle tier, a keyword filter retains sentences with at least two content words shared with the question, falling back to the highest-overlap sentences if too few qualify. It preserves original order. The comparison is restricted to the \pruningDenominator{} cases with both full and pruned outputs. We also include \closedBookCount{} closed-book questions and \literalControlDenominator{} matched literal-versus-paraphrase controls.
+
+\section{Scoring and Comparisons}
+
+Normalized accuracy requires the target code to appear without the competing code. Exact-output compliance requires a response containing one code and no other text. A distractor capture contains the competing code without the target, and an invalid response contains both expected codes or neither. We strip only the runner's fixed transport trailer before applying these rules.
+
+Every accuracy interval is a \confidenceLevel{}\% Wilson score interval. Paired changes are differences in accuracy over an exact intersection of scenario keys. We report wins, losses, and ties for these comparisons because the small denominators make an aggregate difference alone easy to misread. The experiment is descriptive of the frozen model and does not assign sampling uncertainty to decoding, which is deterministic.
+
 \section{Results}
-% TODO(agent): >= 2 figures or tables, all values via macros.
 
+\paragraph{Amount.}
+\textbf{The matched grid does not show accuracy degradation as context grows.} Accuracy was \amountSharedTwoAccuracy{}\% at \tierTwoTokens{} mean tokens and \amountSharedFourAccuracy{}\% at \tierFourTokens{}, a paired change of \amountSharedTwoFourChange{} points over \amountSharedTwoFourDenominator{} cases. It remained \amountSharedEightAccuracy{}\% at \tierEightTokens{}, with \amountSharedFourEightWins{} wins, \amountSharedFourEightLosses{} losses, and \amountSharedFourEightTies{} ties relative to the middle tier (Table~\ref{tab:amount-quality}). The wide intervals and small shared set do not locate a degradation threshold.
+
+\begin{table}[t]
+\caption{Matched amount and filler-quality comparisons. Brackets give \confidenceLevel{}\% Wilson intervals; $n$ is the matched denominator. Changes are later minus earlier for amount and related minus unrelated for quality.}
+\label{tab:amount-quality}
+\centering
+\small
+\begin{tabular}{@{}lccc@{}}
+\toprule
+Comparison & Accuracy A & Accuracy B & Change ($n$) \\
+\midrule
+Amount: short to middle
+& \amountSharedTwoAccuracy{} [\amountSharedTwoAccuracyLow{}, \amountSharedTwoAccuracyHigh{}]
+& \amountSharedFourAccuracy{} [\amountSharedFourAccuracyLow{}, \amountSharedFourAccuracyHigh{}]
+& \amountSharedTwoFourChange{} (\amountSharedTwoFourDenominator{}) \\
+Amount: middle to long
+& \amountSharedFourAccuracy{} [\amountSharedFourAccuracyLow{}, \amountSharedFourAccuracyHigh{}]
+& \amountSharedEightAccuracy{} [\amountSharedEightAccuracyLow{}, \amountSharedEightAccuracyHigh{}]
+& \amountSharedFourEightChange{} (\amountSharedFourEightDenominator{}) \\
+\midrule
+Quality at short tier
+& \qualityTwoIrrelevantAccuracy{} [\qualityTwoIrrelevantAccuracyLow{}, \qualityTwoIrrelevantAccuracyHigh{}]
+& \qualityTwoRelevantAccuracy{} [\qualityTwoRelevantAccuracyLow{}, \qualityTwoRelevantAccuracyHigh{}]
+& \qualityTwoRelevantChange{} (\qualityTwoRelevantDenominator{}) \\
+Quality at middle tier
+& \qualityFourIrrelevantAccuracy{} [\qualityFourIrrelevantAccuracyLow{}, \qualityFourIrrelevantAccuracyHigh{}]
+& \qualityFourRelevantAccuracy{} [\qualityFourRelevantAccuracyLow{}, \qualityFourRelevantAccuracyHigh{}]
+& \qualityFourRelevantChange{} (\qualityFourRelevantDenominator{}) \\
+\bottomrule
+\end{tabular}
+\end{table}
+
+\paragraph{Position.}
+\textbf{Moving the answer clause to the end produced the largest and most consistent gain.} At the shortest tier, accuracy rose from \positionTwoStartAccuracy{}\% at the start to \positionTwoEndAccuracy{}\% at the end, a paired increase of \positionTwoStartEndChange{} points with \positionTwoStartEndWins{} wins and no losses. At the middle tier, the same move raised accuracy by \positionFourStartEndChange{} points with \positionFourStartEndWins{} wins and no losses. The sparse longest tier also favored the end over the start by \positionEightStartEndChange{} points, but it contains only \positionEightStartEndDenominator{} pairs. Middle performance was below end performance at the two populated tiers (Table~\ref{tab:position}).
+
+\begin{table}[t]
+\caption{Accuracy by answer-clause position. Each row compares the same scenario families across positions. Brackets give \confidenceLevel{}\% Wilson intervals.}
+\label{tab:position}
+\centering
+\small
+\begin{tabular}{@{}lccc@{}}
+\toprule
+Mean tokens & Start & Middle & End \\
+\midrule
+\tierTwoTokens{}
+& \positionTwoStartAccuracy{} [\positionTwoStartAccuracyLow{}, \positionTwoStartAccuracyHigh{}]
+& \positionTwoMiddleAccuracy{} [\positionTwoMiddleAccuracyLow{}, \positionTwoMiddleAccuracyHigh{}]
+& \positionTwoEndAccuracy{} [\positionTwoEndAccuracyLow{}, \positionTwoEndAccuracyHigh{}] \\
+\tierFourTokens{}
+& \positionFourStartAccuracy{} [\positionFourStartAccuracyLow{}, \positionFourStartAccuracyHigh{}]
+& \positionFourMiddleAccuracy{} [\positionFourMiddleAccuracyLow{}, \positionFourMiddleAccuracyHigh{}]
+& \positionFourEndAccuracy{} [\positionFourEndAccuracyLow{}, \positionFourEndAccuracyHigh{}] \\
+\tierEightTokens{}
+& \positionEightStartAccuracy{} [\positionEightStartAccuracyLow{}, \positionEightStartAccuracyHigh{}]
+& \positionEightMiddleAccuracy{} [\positionEightMiddleAccuracyLow{}, \positionEightMiddleAccuracyHigh{}]
+& \positionEightEndAccuracy{} [\positionEightEndAccuracyLow{}, \positionEightEndAccuracyHigh{}] \\
+\bottomrule
+\end{tabular}
+\end{table}
+
+\paragraph{Filler quality.}
+\textbf{Topically related filler was more harmful, not safer.} Relative to matched unrelated filler, related business clauses lowered accuracy by \qualityTwoRelevantChange{} points over \qualityTwoRelevantDenominator{} pairs at the shortest tier and by \qualityFourRelevantChange{} points over \qualityFourRelevantDenominator{} pairs at the middle tier. The respective paired outcomes were \qualityTwoRelevantWins{} win, \qualityTwoRelevantLosses{} losses, and \qualityTwoRelevantTies{} ties, then \qualityFourRelevantWins{} win, \qualityFourRelevantLosses{} losses, and \qualityFourRelevantTies{} ties. The longest-tier estimate was \qualityEightRelevantChange{} points, but its \qualityEightRelevantDenominator{} pairs are too sparse to strengthen that conclusion.
+
+\paragraph{Mitigation.}
+\textbf{Keyword pruning backfired on the matched cases.} Full-context accuracy was \pruningFullAccuracy{}\%, compared with \pruningPrunedAccuracy{}\% after filtering, a change of \pruningChange{} points. Only \pruningWins{} case improved; \pruningLosses{} worsened and \pruningTies{} tied (Table~\ref{tab:pruning}). The mechanism follows from the probe design: the filter retains the competing clause because it repeats the question's words, while the paraphrased answer clause has less lexical overlap.
+
+\begin{table}[t]
+\caption{Matched keyword-pruning result and diagnostic controls. Accuracy intervals are \confidenceLevel{}\% Wilson intervals.}
+\label{tab:pruning}
+\centering
+\small
+\begin{tabular}{@{}lcc@{}}
+\toprule
+Condition & Accuracy & Count \\
+\midrule
+Full context
+& \pruningFullAccuracy{} [\pruningFullAccuracyLow{}, \pruningFullAccuracyHigh{}]
+& \pruningFullCount{} \\
+Keyword pruned
+& \pruningPrunedAccuracy{} [\pruningPrunedAccuracyLow{}, \pruningPrunedAccuracyHigh{}]
+& \pruningPrunedCount{} \\
+Literal wording
+& \literalControlAccuracy{} [\literalControlAccuracyLow{}, \literalControlAccuracyHigh{}]
+& \literalControlCount{} \\
+Paraphrase wording
+& \paraphraseControlAccuracy{} [\paraphraseControlAccuracyLow{}, \paraphraseControlAccuracyHigh{}]
+& \paraphraseControlCount{} \\
+Closed book
+& \closedBookAccuracy{} [\closedBookAccuracyLow{}, \closedBookAccuracyHigh{}]
+& \closedBookCount{} \\
+\bottomrule
+\end{tabular}
+\end{table}
+
+The controls support this explanation without proving it for other tasks. Literal wording improved matched accuracy by \literalControlChange{} points, with \literalControlWins{} wins and no losses. Across all outputs, distractor capture was \distractorCaptureRate{}\%, exact-output compliance was \exactOutputComplianceRate{}\%, and invalid output was \invalidOutputRate{}\%. The failures therefore arose mainly from selecting the competing code, not from ignoring the requested format.
